The top toolbar on order detail pages consistently includes "Login as Customer" immediately after "Back" and before "Send Email". This is the only customer-account action present in that position across all observed order detail states.

Evidence: In multiple states of order #000000230 (e.g., evidence #3962, #3972, #3975, #3984, #3986), the toolbar shows: Back, Login as Customer, Send Email, Ship, Reorder. In order #000000065 (evidence #4365), additional buttons appear, but "Login as Customer" remains the first button after "Back" and before "Send Email".

\boxed{Login as Customer}

The correct workflow is to navigate to "My Account > My Orders", where the order table shows the date column, allowing you to locate orders within the desired timeframe. Then clicking "View Order" opens the order detail page, which lists the items ordered, confirming whether the specific item is present. This matches option A.

Evidence: The "My Orders" page (evidence #4233, #4784, etc.) displays a table with columns "Order #", "Date", "Order Total", "Status", "Action". The "Action" column contains "View Order" and "Reorder" links. The order detail pages (e.g., evidence #1589, #3348, #5469) show the "Items Ordered" table with product names. No other options are supported: there is no "Sales > Orders" for customers, no "Advanced Search" for orders, no export, no sort by price, and no "Customers who bought this" feature to infer orders.

\boxed{A}

The Address Book page displays separate sections for "Default Billing Address" and "Default Shipping Address", each with its own independent "Change Billing Address" and "Change Shipping Address" links. There is no indication that changing the billing address overwrites the shipping address. The two addresses are managed independently.

**Answer:** No, clicking "Change Billing Address" does not overwrite the default shipping address.

\boxed{No}

The user is on the Magento admin Orders grid with the target order row visible. To open the shipping-address edit form, the minimum actions are: click the order row to open the order view page, then click the "Edit" link next to the shipping address (evidence #3049, #3050, #3052 show the order view page has an "Edit" link for shipping address). That is 2 actions.

To open the tracking-number entry form for a shipment, the user must first create a shipment. From the order view page, the evidence shows only an "Invoice" button (no "Ship" button) when the order status is "Pending" (evidence #3049, #3050, #3052). Therefore, the user must click "Invoice", then on the invoice page click "Ship" (or "Submit Shipment") to reach the New Shipment page that contains the tracking-number entry fields. This requires at least 3 actions from the Orders grid (click order row, click Invoice, click Ship). Thus, the shipping-address edit form requires fewer actions.

\boxed{B}

The two comment-view tabs above the comments are "Nested" and "Linear".  
Evidence: In [evidence #5865] State 6, lines 317-321, a list contains two links: "Nested" and "Linear", which are the view tabs above the comments section.

\boxed{Nested, Linear}

The question involves a workflow where the user must allocate investments to expense lines based on a protocol. The protocol explicitly states that when the task requires "Total Return of Selected Investments Only," the user should provide the total return in the chat. The chat in the ServiceNow interface corresponds to the "Additional comments (Customer visible)" field. Before finalizing the task by clicking Update, the user must enter the calculated total return into that field. Other options (setting expense line states, explaining methodology, filling work notes, etc.) are not supported by the evidence for this specific task.

Thus, the most meaningful action before clicking Update is to fill the Additional comments field with the total return.

\boxed{B}
